{"ts": "2026-05-03T19:17:58.616339+00:00", "page_id": "p0041", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0041_fn_002", "field": "kind", "before": "citation", "after": "gloss", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-03T19:17:58.616339+00:00", "page_id": "p0041", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0041_fn_002", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": "Starts with the Greek from Nicephorus (Οἱά τις πτηνὸς αἰθέριος...), gives the citation (hist. lib. 3. cap. 1), then adds a second Greek passage from the Greek Menaea (καθάπερ ὑπόπτερός τις...) with its own citation (Jun. 29). Two bird metaphors for Paul — \"like some ethereal bird\" and \"as if winged, running through the whole world\" — gathered together as a little thematic cluster.", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-03T19:17:58.616339+00:00", "page_id": "p0041", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0041_fn_003", "field": "kind", "before": "citation", "after": "gloss", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-03T19:17:58.616339+00:00", "page_id": "p0041", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0041_fn_003", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": " Starts with Nicephorus in Greek (hist. eccles. lib. 12. cap. 34), then adds Chrysostom in Latin (homil. 4. de laudib. Pauli), then Clement of Rome in Greek with a full Latin translation (epistola ad Corinthios pag. 8). Three authorities stacked to corroborate Paul's thirty-five years of ministry and his reaching \"the limit of the West.\"", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
